# Which Decree Restored and Built Jerusalem? (Video Summary)

The video argues that the only decree fitting both the **chronological** and **legal** requirements of Daniel 9:25 is the **Artaxerxes decree of 457 BC**, not because the decree *mentions* rebuilding, but because the *results* (moza) of the decree went into effect when Ezra implemented it in Jerusalem.  
It reframes Daniel 9:25’s “going forth” as the **effective implementation** of the decree, not the date it was written.

**Source:** https://youtu.be/u1WIV5u8txs

---

## Cyrus’ First Decree: 538 BC

> “The first decree is given by Cyrus in the year 538 BC… this is too soon.” (4:41–5:04)

The speaker notes that counting 483 years from 538 BC lands around 55 BC, far too early to reach the Messiah. Therefore, this decree is chronologically disqualified.

---

## Darius’ Decree: 520 BC

> “If we calculate 483 years from 520 BC… this will take us to about 37 BC.” (5:13–5:30)

Although closer, this still does not reach the lifetime of Jesus. Thus, the decree of Darius (Darius I / “Das”) is also excluded on timing alone.

---

## Artaxerxes’ First Decree (Ezra 7): 457 BC

> “If we calculate this date from 457 BC it takes us to about the year 27 AD.” (5:59–6:06)

This is the only decree whose 483‑year span reaches the time of Christ.  
However, critics argue Ezra 7 does not explicitly mention rebuilding Jerusalem. The speaker acknowledges this but claims the key is **when the decree went into effect**, not the wording.

---

## Nehemiah’s Permission: 444/445 BC

> “Calculating the going forth of this decree… takes us to about the year 40 AD.” (6:31–6:52)

Nehemiah’s authorization is too late to reach the Messiah.  
Additionally, the speaker argues Nehemiah received **permission**, not a **decree**, and it concerned **walls**, not the city itself.

---

## Ezra 7 and the “Missing” Rebuilding Language

> “Do you see anything in Ezra 7 that refers to the rebuilding of Jerusalem?… It’s not there.” (10:03–10:58)

The speaker concedes that Ezra 7 contains **restoration** (judges, magistrates) but not explicit **building** language.  
This leads to the central interpretive move: Daniel 9:25’s “going forth” (moza) refers to the **results** of the decree once implemented.

---

## The Key Argument: “Going Forth” = *Moza* (Result), Not *Yatza* (Action)

> “Moza can be a source or a product… the outcome or result of an action.” (21:15–22:04)

The speaker argues:
- *Yatza* = the act of issuing a command (Dan 9:23)
- *Moza* = the **result** or **effect** of a command (Dan 9:25)

Thus, the decree “goes forth” not when written, but when its **effects begin** — when Ezra arrives in Jerusalem and implements it.

---

## Ellen White’s Statement: The Decree Went Into Effect in Autumn 457 BC

> “The decree of Artaxerxes went into effect in the autumn of 457 BC.” (16:54–17:02)

The speaker uses this to argue:
- Artaxerxes wrote the decree in spring 457 BC.
- It **went into effect** when Ezra arrived in Jerusalem in the **fifth month** (August/September).
- This matches the autumn fulfillment of the 2300 days in 1844.

---

## The Law of the Medes and Persians: Decrees Cannot Be Changed

> “The laws of the Medes and Persians could not be changed.” (25:00–26:42)

This principle is used to argue:
- Artaxerxes’ earlier anti‑Jerusalem decree (Ezra 4:7–23) could not be revoked.
- Therefore, his later decree (Ezra 7) must have **implicitly** authorized rebuilding by its *results*, not its wording.
- The “narrative” of Ezra–Nehemiah shows the decree’s effects unfolding over time.

---

## Summary

| Date | Event | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 538 BC | Cyrus’ first decree | Too early; ends ~55 BC |
| 520 BC | Darius’ decree | Ends ~37 BC; still too early |
| **457 BC** | **Artaxerxes’ decree (Ezra 7)** | Only decree reaching Messiah (27 AD); “going forth” = implementation |
| 444/445 BC | Nehemiah’s authorization | Too late; permission, not decree; walls only |
| Autumn 457 BC | Decree “goes forth” (moza) | Ezra implements decree; effects begin |
| 1844 AD | End of 2300 days | Parallels autumn start of 457 BC |

**Commentary on significance to the New Covenant:**  
The argument positions Daniel 9:25–27 as a Messianic prophecy anchored in the 457 BC decree. By tying the “going forth” to the *effect* of the decree rather than its wording, the speaker reinforces the traditional Adventist timeline leading to Christ’s ministry (27 AD) and the later fulfillment of the 2300 days (1844). This interpretation frames the New Covenant as inaugurated precisely within the prophetic structure of Daniel’s timeline.

